A||N||8||Ditz88GT||Anyone install their own alarm system?||||5||
Z||000000||Ditz88GT||05-07-2001||03:21 PM||||I just got an alarm for my Fiero.  I called around to have it installed, but they want $160 (on avg.) to do it.  I've installed lots of stereos, but never an alarm.  Any hints would be great.  Or if it is really hard to get right, should I just pay the $160?<P>BTW: It's an Avital Hurricane and an 88 GT.<P>Thanks<BR>Ditz88GT||||reg||5
Z||000001||grinthock||05-07-2001||04:15 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Ditz88GT:<BR><B>I just got an alarm for my Fiero.  I called around to have it installed, but they want $160 (on avg.) to do it.  I've installed lots of stereos, but never an alarm.  Any hints would be great.  Or if it is really hard to get right, should I just pay the $160?<P>BTW: It's an Avital Hurricane and an 88 GT.<P>Thanks<BR>Ditz88GT</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I have a HUGE comment on this, I did mine, I have done it once RIGHT and once quick, it took me 3 hours to do it quick and even more to do it right, AND it was a pain, mine included install of door motors, and a SH1T load of wiring!!!<P><BR>Do yourself a fav, DON'T do it yourself<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||05-07-2001||04:25 PM||||it all depends on what features the alarm has. there are as many alarms as there are car types. The simpler ones that only connect to battery hot and ground are ez. they just use motion sensing and current draw when dome lite comes on. When you get power remote locks, pager, etc, gets whole lot more complicated. best left to pros, that way you get a warranty too.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||05-07-2001||04:33 PM||||When I bought mine, the company required the alarm be installed by one of their dealers. So I had the dealer install it since it was part of the purchase price anyway. I have since rewired the entire system and relocated the "brain" and all other components to "safer" locations in the car. If I had the opportunity, I would do it myself, but if you're not into tearing into the wiring of your car, leave it to someone else.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-07-2001||06:21 PM||||I had an alarm installed once. They don't solder connections and they placed the brain box in an easy spot to see it. I had all kinds of problems with it. From then on I install them myself. It isn't hard to install a standard alarm system with keyless entry. Takes a few hours to run wires and such but, It was installed very well and haven't had a problem with it for over 5 years. I have installed many in various vehicles and they are not hard.<P>BTW ~ If you install it yourself, it is nice because if something should go wrong you'll know where everything is to fix it.<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 05-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Taijiguy||05-07-2001||08:01 PM||||I would say it depends strictly on your *actual* skill level. If you have basic woring skills, and can read the Fiero wiring drawing in the Haynes manual, and the schematic for the alarm, you should have no trouble. It also helps if you have a touch of creativity as well, to throw into the mix, since there will always be stuff you don't expect and can't be predicted. <BR>I installed mine with lock, and window controls (both up and down) and it took several hours. But as has been mentioned here, you can solder and shrink tube all the connections, where the alarm installers will generally just Scotch-lock, wire nut, and tape the connections. (not good enough for me) And you will know where all the connections, components, and wiring are if there's ever a problem.<BR>Plus, you have a hell of a resource here if you hit a snag.<BR>~Taiji||||reg||1
Z||000006||Spektyr||05-08-2001||01:20 PM||||I had an Excalibur alarm installed on my mild street Nissan Pickup.  The techs wedged the alarm brain up behind the dash where it sticks down in the middle, not actually mounting it to anything.  The shock sensor was zip-tied to the steering column.<P>I never had any trouble with the alarm system, but in retrospect that was a bit surprising.  If install hadn't been free I probably would've done it myself as I'm one of those weird people that can read wiring diagrams as easily as English and I have professional grade electrical and electronics tools.  (But then I was a Nuclear Field Electronics Tech in the Navy, so that's about par for the course.)<P>When my Fiero gets her alarm, I'll probably do it myself, unless install is free or damn cheap.  If I have it done by someone else, it'll be just to save me a little time so I can go through the car and fix all the little details they don't think of, or don't care to do right.  (Butt-connectors are NOT ok... soldered connections with shrink wrap are.)<P>The alarm is the second to last line of defense against my car being stolen (my .357 being the last line) so for me to be happy, it has to be done 100% right.<P>If you feel confident installing the alarm and poking through the wiring of your Fiero, then I'd say go for it.  Take your time and do it right.  If you don't, then have them install it, ask them to show you where all the components are, then drive it home and trace every wire and fix the connections.  If you can't solder, find someone who can.  The interior of a car isn't a good place to practice.  Molten solder will ruin carpet and just about anything else it touches in the car.<P>------------------<BR>85 GT (to be modified...)<BR>--Um, no the paint isn't oxidized, that's the ultra-rare Whirlpool White textured finish... yeah.<BR>89 Lincoln Town Car (has Fiero Envy)<BR>--Hey!  Can I have an anti-sway bar too?||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||05-08-2001||03:22 PM||||I installed mine, with keyless entry, dome light illumination, power trunk pop, ignition controlled locks, starter kill, and dual stage shock sensor.<P>It wasnt hard at all, but I am pretty good with electronics anyway.  (60% of all the work I have done on my Fiero has been electrically related).  If you are going to wire in power locks, you'll probably need to get some automotive relays (bosch type).  My alarm needed 2 relays for the locks, 1 for the trunk pop, 1 for the dome light, and 1 for the starter kill.  Places like radio shack sell these relays for about $5.99 ea.  I get mine from <A HREF="http://www.partsexpress.com" TARGET=_blank>www.partsexpress.com</A>    Generic brand, bosch type, 30 amp automotive relays sell there for $.99 ea, and are sometimes on special for .89 ea.  They charge a lot for shipping, but I needed a whole lot of them so it was worth it.<P>And like was said here before, if you do decide to do it yourself, many of us here can help.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/joesig_1.jpg"> <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ditz88GT||05-08-2001||05:11 PM||||FieroJoe,<P>You said you needed relays for the power locks.  My alarm says I may be able to avoid that if I know if the locks are Positive Trigger, Negative Trigger or Reverse Polarity.  So, I guess my question is, do you are anyone else know which it is?  If it's reverse Polarity I will need the two relays, but if it's Pos. or Neg., I won't need these for this alarm.<P>Thanks for all your help,<BR>Ditz88GT<p>[This message has been edited by Ditz88GT (edited 05-08-2001).]||||reg||5
